e political speech  meaningful so it broadened it rather  directly but one of the things that I  want to focus on tonight is the idea  that many people will argue that the  first amendment limits start with speech  that we consider speech to be of no  value this is an argument that we see  recurring again and again and a good  example of that comes with Justice John  Paul Stevens in actually his first  decision as a Supreme Court judge  a case involving zoning of adult adult  uses young versus American mini theatres  in 1974 and in that case he basically  said few of us would send our sons and  daughters off to war to preserve a  citizen's right to see specified sexual  activities exhibited in theatres of our  choice now that to him was sort of a  limited the first amendment that was  part of a plow a per lap that's easy for  you to say a plurality decision meaning  it wasn't the decision of the court but  that sentiment is something that you see  expressed again and again although  interestingly justice Potter Stewart who  famously wrote I may not be able to  intelligibly define obscenity but I know  it when I see it disagreed with that  sentiment and wrote in a dissenting  opinion that if the First Amendment  protects no more than what we would send  people off to defend then it is  circumscribed by public opinion and he  wasn't going to sign on to that  sentiment we see as I mentioned this  argument being made again and again the  FCC for example in enforcing its  indecency regulation says that it can  look at the context of speech when it's  broadcast and say that by determining  whether or not the speech is of  sufficient merit in its view whether or  not the words being used in that context  are protected or whether they're not and  the very same words may be protected or  they may be the subject of severe  penalties depending on the FCC's  determination of the context there are a  number of cases that have recently been  decided by the Supreme Court some that