s  that they make and if we give the  government the power to criminalize not  even the acts but speech about the acts  then it is an act that is deeply  offensive to the First Amendment now  again this trial took place last summer  this was the the legal team I was very  privileged to serve as local counsel in  a case that involved I think some of the  finest First Amendment lawyers in the  country  Louise Sirkin Paul cambria al Gelbart  and of course we have John here now at  the time this was taken we were looking  a little bit more than long we just been  in the midst of jury selection and so we  didn't know what the outcome of the case  was going to be at that  point this was taken immediately after  the verdict after the after judge Leon  throughout the the government's case  without our having to put on a defense  and so there are a lot more smiles here  this was taken in front of a statue of  Blackstone in front of the courthouse in  DC and so again one of the things it was  very heartening about that is that even  though many of the rulings on motions  had been unfavorable ultimately the  important motion to dismiss the case was  granted and also it was fairly revealing  to us that for members of the press that  spoke to jurors after the case the  jurors basically said we would not have  convicted him that if there is a  community standard in this country it is  let your neighbor do what he wants it's  not our business now this brings up  another case where you might wonder  where the First Amendment value as this  is the case that was mentioned in the  introduction involving phenomenon called  crush videos if you don't know what they  are I probably shouldn't go into any  great detail let's just say that they  involve women wearing stilettos doing  awful things to woodland animals the law  however that was adopted had a was was  what far beyond the issue of crush  videos in 1999 Congress adopted a law to  prohibit not the actions because animal  cruelty is