ve fights  that he was involved in he doesn't fight  dogs he didn't even film the the fights  they were filmed by others some of them  in Japan where dogfighting is legal some  of them decades earlier who simply  incorporated them into these videos and  so the very first prosecution under this  law adopted for Kraus videos was against  mr. Stevens 69 years old a federal SWAT  team broke down his door at 5:00 in the  morning  roust and hand his wife out of bed the  wife with a heart condition  and put him on trial he was convicted he  was sentenced to 37 months in prison  which actually snaps into perspective a  little bit when you realize that michael  vick who actually was running a  dogfighting ring was sentenced to 23  months but for putting together images  of dogfighting mr. Stevens was sentenced  to 37 months the Court of Appeals struck  down the conviction on First Amendment  grounds and it went to the United States  Supreme Court where I was  again privileged to work on that that  defense team and interestingly the  argument that the government made in  that case in support of the law was one  of the most frightening things I've seen  in my years as a First Amendment lawyer  they essentially argued that if the  government can show that speech is of no  value and that it might be harmful then  that speech is not protected by the  First Amendment at all ultimately the  Supreme Court disagreed and in an eight  to one decision said that it is  protected by the First Amendment is  excluded from the First Amendment and  described the government's theory as  being far-reaching and frightening a  fortunately a view of the First  Amendment that the Roberts Court has  continued to to uphold in cases after  that that case I think is the beginning  of a trend that we've seen in cases like  this where the government has made the  argument that there are additional  categories of speech that shouldn't be  protected by the First Amendment that  also came up again Jean mentioned in the  introduction involving funeral protests  of the Westboro Baptist Church it was  the argument was made that their  particular message which is hateful and  I think inexplicable is something that  shouldn't be protected by the First  Amendment  actually and they provoked all kinds of  reactions and we'll see a few more of  those and that's part of why the speech  is protected they're essentially a  family cult their view  is that all catastrophes that we face  deaths overseas and foreign wars are  God's retribution for America's  tolerance of homosexuality and so they  have adopted a number of techniques for  their protests including protesting in  the vicinity of military funerals now  there's something that is really not  known as well about their their form of  protest is that they're actually fairly  smart about it in that they call the  police in advance they follow all police  directives they keep the distance from  the funeral and in the case that went to  the Supreme Court they were not even  seen by the family about it by the  father who finally ultimately brought  suit until later he saw some of the  signs on television the popular  misconception is that they were there  disrupting the funeral and that really  wasn't the case I filed an amicus brief  in that case on behalf of the reporters  committee for freedom of the press and  2021 other news organizations including  such places as Dow Jones New York Times  NPR and so on because the underlying  rule First Amendment values that would  have been disrupted because of allowing  a suit to go forward against these folks  would have been devastating to the media  to First Amendment values in general  now the argument again the government  made and also the amici that supported  I'm sure not the government of the the  Snyder family made and actually 46  states filed amicus briefs in support of  the family was that again the message  that the Westboro Baptist Church brings  to us simply isn't worth it and  shouldn't be protected by the First  Amendment but again this misunderstands  the marketplace of ideas  so once person's message doesn't define  whether or not there's value in a given  communication because after all a  community the act of communication is  two-sided  there's also what is heard and what is  believed some people will see that and  think hmm maybe so most people I think  the vast majority people will see that  and say what are these people thinking  how can anyone have these ideas and  that's the marketplace of ideas  in in action in addition there is  counter speech and as you probably know  the Westboro Baptist Church tends to  provoke a lot of counter speech when  they show up anywhere to demonstrate  that is the marketplace of ideas in  action and if you don't believe that  now this isn't real of course I I just  thought it was funny but there's there's  actually a website out there where you  can go on and get this sort of  background and you can add whatever  message you want you know happy birthday  mom just whatever you'd like and you're  sponsored by the Westboro Baptist Church  but again it is the marketplace of ideas  in action now as I say this kind of  argument that we've seen in cases  involving such disgusting things as  crushed videos and silly ideas like  those espoused by the West bette  Westboro Baptist Church pop up in all  kinds of other situations as some of the  people my age in the audience an older  will remember Lenny Bruce was placed on  trial for obscenity because of his  stand-up act in three different states  in California and Illinois and in New  York he was convicted in Illinois and in  New York the conviction in Illinois was  overturned the conviction in New York  was not because Lenny died before the  conviction could be perfected George  Carlin similarly before his troubles  with the FCC was arrested for obscenity  at the Wisconsin State Fair think about  it stand-up comics being arrested in  this country for word crimes it simply  doesn't sound American and yet we have  to continue to fight these First  Amendment battles because of the  argument that is made is that this is  just trivial speech it's just jokes and  yet they're saying bad things that the  community doesn't like therefore we  should be able to make them criminals in  the case of these we have one happy  ending so far although as I say Lenny  was dead so it's not so happy for him  and we're still fighting the second one  the first one involves a posthumous  pardon that we got for Lenny Bruce in  2003 in the state of New York we  petitioned the Governor George Pataki  for a posthumous pardon and who would  have thought it would happen he granted  it at the end of 2003 basically arguing  that it's time to set the record right  for First Amendment purposes this is  Kitty Bruce his daughter and this is the  certificate from Governor Pataki we were  later celebrating was when the authors  of the book that led to the pardon  effort Ron Collins and David's cover and  book is the trials of Lenny Bruce go by  it they won the Hugh Hefner First  Amendment award that year for the book  that led to the pardon effort and that's  where I first met Katie face to face  although the day of the pardon was a  very strange day it was right before  Christmas and I remember I gotten a  little bit of warning from the  governor's office that it was going to  come down and then the press calls  started coming in and toward the end of  the day I got a call and a woman on the  other end said I know we haven't met but  I want to thank you for all you've done  my name is Katie Bruce and I keep  getting calls from reporters and they  asked me what I should tell them about  you and so I said to her well tell them  I represent living people too and  and but anyway it was quite a  celebration and you know while not much  help to Lenny Bruce I think it is  helpful to the rest of us to have a  state government officially acknowledge  30-so