ng  on for years as I mentioned and two of  them have actually been decided by  courts of appeals the United States  Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit  held that the FCC's indecency policy in  the Fox case and in the NYPD blue case  was unconstitutionally vague that the  more the FCC has to say about what it  means the less anybody understands it  and so as a result it's unconstitutional  the Superbowl case were still waiting we  have one decision from the US Court of  Appeals from the Third Circuit where it  was struck down then after an  intervening Supreme Court case they sent  it back to the Third Circuit and said  try over because the the Third Circuit  has looked at it on administrative law  grounds and I think next we'll be  finding out whether or not the Third  Circuit will look at it as a  constitutional matter but meanwhile  these cases continue to go on and even  though we have had these positive steps  these positive decisions they still  continue to take their toll for example  in 2007 it was the 50th anniversary of  the the poem howl being acquitted on  obscenity charges Allen Ginsberg was was  charged with obscenity because of a  public reading of the poem how he was  acquitted 50 years later because of the  FCC rules stations felt they still  couldn't put it on the air because  they're nervous about what the FCC might  do got a little bit of coverage and an  editorial of New York Times and an  editorial cartoon but nonetheless  stations are still nervous even two  weeks ago for those of you who aren't  from Washington they might not have seen  this story a woman went into the  National Gallery of Art and attacked the  one hundred and twelve year old Gauguin  painting to Tahitian women she said it  was an evil painting because it depicted  nudity and because obviously with two  women standing there it had a lesbian  and so as a result she had to do  something about it she tried to rip it  from the wall and fortunately it was  covered with plexiglass so she couldn't  do any damage to it but she beat on the  plexiglass with her fists now she she  also said that she was from the CIA and  had radio in her head so she's now being  fitted for tinfoil hat but nonetheless  the damage from the First Amendment  perspective is even with an obviously  newsworthy story like this news stations  were afraid to cover the the episode  uncensored this is what WJLA in  washington did they had a banner go  across the middle of the women's chests  so that you would not see breasts or or  nipples FoxNews blurred out they the  breasts others handled it in different  ways but even with two affirmative Court  of Appeals decisions saying that the  rules are unconstitutional because they  are vague even with the FCC itself  saying that certain times on the news  will say it's okay we're just not going  to tell you when we're not going to say  it's okay because of that stations are  nervous because the the penalties for  violating these FCC rules are really  very very hefty and there's it can be in  certain cases a lose your license kind  of violation there's one more case  that's still pending at the Supreme  Court and this may be oddly enough one  of the most important of them all  this is Swartz negative versus in the  electronic Merchants Association a case  involving the California law prohibiting  the sale or rental of violent video  games to young people the courts of  appeals have uniformly struck down these  laws as being a violation of the First  Amendment the Supreme Court took it up  and as heard argument we simply haven't  heard what the outcome is going to be  yet the reason it's so important is that  California is really pushing the  envelope and arguing three different  things that arguing first that violent  video games should be a new category of  unprotected speech because it's just not  important secondly they argue that kids  under 18 really don't have any First  Amendment rights anyway and then thirdly  they're 