kwards to protect editorial judgment  at least in the news context but such  assurances are not sufficient instead  the FCC should bend over backwards to  create a standard that gives  broadcasters that notice that is  required by the First Amendment and then  finally a case that I haven't talked  about tonight but that I think  establishes these these principles this  is a case I argued the Supreme Court  about ten years ago involving Believe It  or Not the phenomenon of  signal bleed signal bleed meaning random  images of unscrambled cable that can  somehow be discerned along with it has  also been called Picasso porn and as  part of the Telecommunications Act a  number of years ago heavy regulations  were placed on adult cable networks in  particularly playboy television the  court in a five-to-four decision said  that we cannot be influenced by the  perception that the regulation in  question is not a major one because the  speech is not very important the history  of the law of free expression is one of  indication in cases involving speech  that many citizens find shabby offensive  or even ugly and finally don't bother to  try and read this next when you'll go  blind there's a lot of words here but  this is the reason I'm a First Amendment  lawyer this was in the majority opinion  in Playboy Justice Kennedy wrote this  for the court when a student first  encounters our free speech jurisprudence  he or she might think that it is  influenced by the philosophy that one  idea is as good as any other and that if  are in the literature objective  standards of style taste decorum beauty  and aesthetics are deemed by the  Constitution to be inappropriate indeed  unattainable quite the opposite is true  the Constitution no more enforces a  relativistic philosophy of moral  nihilism than it does any other point of  view the Constitution exists precisely  so that opinions and judgments including  aesthetic and moral judgments about art  and literature can be formed tested and  expressed once the Constitution says is  that these judgments are for the  individual to make not for the  government to decree even with the  mandate or approval of majority to me  you can find no better statement of what  the First Amendment is about and I can  tell you from the standpoint of having  fought that case for a number of years  and finally argued in the Supreme Court  there is no more humbling experience  than to see the work that you have done  end up on a page with words like that  and for that reason I feel like the work  that I do every day is a great privilege  thank you  you
 

